 RBI Policy Preview: Still prefer RBI to hold rates, drain liquidity first and signal intent via change in stance
 The current growth-inflation outlook and liquidity conditions call for a status quo on rates, alongside a shift in the monetary policy stance from neutral to a tightening bias, for three reasons.
 Vikram Chhabra
 October 04, 2026 / 07:17 IST 
 --> 
 RBI Monetary Policy Preview 
 Still prefer RBI to hold rates, drain liquidity first and signal its intent through change in stance
 Status quo on rates gives RBI time to drain excess liquidity and improve transmission
 RBI may announce another Rs 1 trillion of G-sec sales to improve transmission
 Did our AI summary help? 
 Market Mastery
On the surface, India's growth story looks as resilient as ever. Q1FY27 GDP grew 7.8 percent YoY, comfortably ahead of expectations, and leading indicators, such as credit growth, auto sales, industrial production and foreign trade, suggest that momentum has carried into Q2 FY27. But the risks to growth have intensified. The deadlock in the US-Iran talks is weighing on energy supplies, the monsoon has disappointed, and the lift from GST rationalisation is fading as the base turns adverse. Put together, we expect growth to slow to 6-6.5 percent YoY in H2, from 7 percent+ in H1.
Inflation, for its part, has been drifting higher, with early signs of broadening. Our preferred gauge of underlying inflation, CPI excluding food, fuel and precious metals, has risen from 2.1 percent YoY in January 2026 to 2.9 percent in August. The direction is up, but the level is still comfortably below the RBI's 4 percent target.
Here too, however, the risks are tilted to the upside. Kharif sowing has been broadly normal, but the weak monsoon threatens crop yields, and poor reservoir levels in northern and southern India could weigh on rabi output.
In addition, if energy prices stay elevated, pass-through to broader prices will follow with a lag. In our base case, we expect headline CPI inflation to move closer to the RBI's 6 percent upper tolerance limit in the coming months, before easing thereafter. But the trajectory could shift higher if any of these risks materialise.
Meanwhile, FCNR(B) inflows of US$133 billion have pushed banking system liquidity into a large surplus, driving overnight rates below the policy floor, the 5 percent standing deposit facility (SDF) rate, in the first half of September 2026. Rates have since recovered, helped by the RBI's FX sell-buy swaps, its sale of Rs 1 trillion of government securities (G-secs) and a temporary drain from advance tax payments. But they still hover closer to the SDF rate than to the repo rate.
Taken together, we believe the current growth-inflation outlook and liquidity conditions call for a status quo on rates, alongside a shift in the monetary policy stance from neutral to a tightening bias, for three reasons.
First, it gives the RBI time to drain excess liquidity and improve transmission, since ample liquidity could blunt the impact of a rate hike. Second, the external situation and energy prices remain too volatile to act on with confidence, and the monsoon's impact will become clearer as kharif crops reach the market over the next 1-2 months. Third, a change in stance would still signal to the market that anchoring inflation takes precedence over growth, without the risk of a premature hike.
That's our prescription. Our prediction, however, is a different matter.
We believe a 25 basis point hike in the policy repo rate will be the path of least resistance for the RBI. With the Fed, the ECB and the Bank of Japan all hiking, the RBI may not want to be the odd one out, especially with the INR down 8 percent over the past year. The last thing it would want is to give speculators a reason to bet against the rupee. And with excess liquidity still a problem, the RBI may also announce another Rs 1 trillion of G-sec sales to improve monetary policy transmission.
We would still prefer the RBI to hold rates, drain liquidity first and signal its intent through a change in stance. But with global central banks hiking and the rupee under pressure, a hike may simply be the easier call to make, even if it isn't the right one.
